_Focus: Surface mentions of competitive threats in core segments (e.g., power, aviation) including pricing dynamics, new entrants, or technology displacement risks._

## Findings
- **General Electric (Conglomerate, Q1 2020)**: Aviation segment faces severe competitive and demand pressures due to COVID-19, with global departures down ~75% and shop visits down ~60%. Military aviation remains strong.
- **General Electric (Conglomerate, Q1 2020)**: Power segment experiences mixed results, with gas turbine deliveries impacted by supply chain disruptions and lower electricity demand.
- **General Electric (Conglomerant, Q1 2020)**: Healthcare segment sees increased demand for COVID-19 related medical equipment but reduced demand for non-essential procedures.

## Implications
- Immediate cost actions (e.g., 10% workforce reduction in aviation) are critical to mitigate near-term margin pressures.
- Accelerated digital transformation in healthcare (e.g., Mural Virtual Care Solution) positions GE for post-pandemic recovery.
- Aviation's competitive position remains strong long-term due to youngest installed base of engines, but short-term volatility requires active customer collaboration.

## Key findings
- Aviation commercial services orders down 4%, with shop visits down ~60% YoY due to COVID-19 travel restrictions.
- Power gas turbine deliveries tracking to 45-50 units in 2020 despite IPP pressures in US/Mexico and oil price impacts in Middle East/SSA.
- Healthcare equipment orders surged 40% (ex-Biopharma) with ventilator production doubling and plans to double again by June 2020.
- Aviation deferred maintenance and lower utilization pressuring CSA margins, with full-year 2020 recovery dependent on air travel rebound.
- Renewables revenue up 28% YoY but profitability impacted by supply chain disruptions and execution challenges in grid/hydro segments.
- GE Capital facing elevated repossessions and lease restructurings with ~80% of customers seeking short-term deferrals.

